import TextPage from '../layouts/TextPage.astro';
import { ALL_TOOLS } from '../lib/tools';
---

<TextPage
  title="About RosterOwl — who makes it and why | RosterOwl"
  description="RosterOwl is a free suite of classroom tools built by a small independent studio. No accounts, no ads, no student data collection — everything runs in your browser."
  heading="About RosterOwl"
  lede="RosterOwl is a set of <strong>free classroom tools that share one class list</strong>. Paste your roster once, then use it for seating charts, groups, name tags, certificates and the rest — without it ever leaving your browser."
>
  <h2>Why it exists</h2>
  <p>
    Every teacher rebuilds the same class list over and over — once in the seating chart tool, again
    in a group generator, again in a spreadsheet for the attendance grid, again when the substitute
    needs a copy. The list is the same every time. Only the piece of paper changes.
  </p>
  <p>
    So RosterOwl is built the other way around: the roster is the thing you own, and the {ALL_TOOLS.length}
    tools are just different ways of printing it. Set up your room and your keep-apart rules once,
    and every tool already knows about them.
  </p>

  <h2>Who makes it</h2>
  <p>
    RosterOwl is built and run by <strong>KNG Technologies</strong>, a small independent software
    studio. It isn't venture-funded, it isn't a side project of a bigger edtech platform, and it has
    no sales team who will call your district. It's one team shipping tools for a job we think is
    badly served.
  </p>
  <p>
    You can <a href="/contact/">reach a human</a> — the address goes to the people who write the
    code, not to a ticket queue.
  </p>

  <h2>How it's free</h2>
  <p>
    There are no ads, no trackers, no email wall, and no "free for 30 students" ceiling. There is no
    paid tier today. If one ever appears it will be for extras — premium certificate designs, opt-in
    cloud sync — and <strong>the tools that are free today stay free and uncapped</strong>. That's a
    commitment, not a promotion.
  </p>

  <h2 id="student-data">Student data, FERPA and COPPA</h2>
  <p>
    This section is here for the person in your district who has to sign off on new tools. The short
    version:
  </p>
  <div class="callout">
    <p>
      <strong>Used as it comes, RosterOwl receives no student data.</strong> No account, no upload,
      no server that processes what you type. Student names are handled entirely by your own browser
      and stored only on the device you typed them into. Every tool on the site works this way, and
      that is the default state for every teacher.
    </p>
  </div>
  <p>
    There is exactly one exception, and a teacher has to go and switch it on: <strong>sync</strong>.
    If a teacher signs in with a Google account, the class list they have typed is stored on our
    infrastructure (a Cloudflare database) so they can continue on a second computer. That copy is
    ordinary text, so it is readable by us in principle; it can be deleted by the teacher at any
    time from the sync menu, and deleting it leaves their own device untouched.
  </p>
  <p>
    So the FERPA answer depends on which of those two states your staff use, and we would rather set
    that out plainly than give you a single sentence that is only true half the time. With sync off,
    no education record reaches us, there is nothing for us to disclose, and no retention period to
    negotiate. With sync on, we hold the class list a teacher chose to upload — names only, never
    absences, tags or notes — for as long as they keep the account, and we delete it on request or
    when they press the button themselves. Under COPPA we collect no information from children
    directly in either state; the only account RosterOwl ever creates belongs to an adult teacher.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your district's answer is "no cloud storage of student names", that is a supported
    configuration rather than a refusal: tell your staff not to sign in, and every tool still works
    exactly as documented.
  </p>
  <p>
    We would rather you verified that than took our word for it.
    <a href="/privacy/">Our privacy page</a> lists every network request the site makes and walks
    you through checking it yourself in about two minutes — including the part where you turn your
    Wi-Fi off and the tools keep working.
  </p>
  <p>
    If your district needs this in a particular form — a completed vendor questionnaire, a signed
    data-privacy agreement, a statement on letterhead — <a href="/contact/">ask us</a>. We'd rather
    fill in the form than lose you to a procurement checkbox.
  </p>

  <h2>Accessibility</h2>
  <p>
    The interface is set in Atkinson Hyperlegible, a typeface designed by the Braille Institute for
    readers with low vision. Every control is reachable by keyboard, the whole site works with a
    screen reader, there's a dark theme for projector-lit rooms, and motion respects your system's
    reduced-motion setting. If something doesn't work with your setup, that's a bug — please
    <a href="/contact/">tell us</a>.
  </p>

  <h2>What's next</h2>
  <p>
    The suite grows in the direction the stored roster makes better. Substitute binders, more
    certificate occasions, and offline install are on the list. Things that don't touch the class
    list — behavior analytics, gradebooks, parent messaging — are deliberately not.
  </p>
</TextPage>
